s a reference\n  section for items 06 and 09, which carry the mathematics. Write §10.\n\n- [ ] **11 The write-up: `reports/subexp_pathwidth.md` §0, the note, the CLAUDE.md paragraph (drafted, not applied).**\n  §0 in two pages, in the stance of TASK.md: the algorithms this loop\n  produced with their running times and their Lean names first; the\n  crossover; what is unconditional and what is `SubexpCount →`; the\n  question at `k = Θ(n)` as it now stands; what is not claimed. A note\n  *Pathwidth after ETH* as `paper3/pathwidth_after_eth.md` (prose only)\n  organised by algorithm, not by map. `literature/MISSING.md` entries for\n  every paper cited but not held. The CLAUDE.md paragraph in the report's\n  last section, **not applied**. Run the full gate.\n\n- [ ] **12 Reserve.** The algebraic question item 05 leaves sharpest —\n  whether a sum of products over a cube counts width-`k` layouts of a\n  class (the cobipartite cut condition, the twin-class structure) in the\n  paper's framework, as an algorithm with a proof or a stated obstacle,\n  one session with its own kill; or whichever of 06–09 ended short.\n----\nreports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/:\n01_audit\n02_frame\n03_reductions\n04_classes\n05_chain\n\nreports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/01_audit/:\n2006-Bodlaender-Fomin-Koster-Kratsch-Thilikos-Exact-Algorithms-Treewidth-TR-UU-CS-2006-032.txt\n2006-Fomin-Hoie-Pathwidth-Cubic-Graphs-Exact-Algorithms-IPL.txt\n2009-Suchan-Villanger-Computing-Pathwidth-Faster-Than-2n-IWPEC.txt\n2012-Bodlaender-Fomin-Koster-Kratsch-Thilikos-Note-Exact-Algorithms-Vertex-Ordering-TOCS.txt\n2016-Kitsunai-Kobayashi-Komuro-Tamaki-Tano-Computing-Directed-Pathwidth-1.89n-Algorithmica.txt\n2021-Kobayashi-Nakahata-Note-Exponential-Time-Algorithms-Linearwidth-arXiv.txt\n2023-Bansal-Katzelnick-On-Approximating-Cutwidth-and-Pathwidth-arXiv.txt\n2024-Bodlaender-Approximation-Algorithms-Treewidth-Pathwidth-Treedepth-Short-Survey-WG.txt\n2025-Exponential-Time-Approximation-Schemes-Vertex-Ordering-ITCS.txt\n2026-Kaneda-Kobayashi-Tamaki-Fast-Practical-Single-Exponential-Algorithms-Branchwidth-arXiv.txt\nREADME.md\nbonnet_hdr.txt\neth.txt\nsecond_reading.md\n----\nFrame.lean\nPrimitiveMachine.lean\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Regression gate for loop0014 (pathwidth after ETH).\n\nPasses only if all five hold:\n1. `lake build` in lean/ succeeds;\n2. every `.lean` file under lean/MOSPFormalization/ (top level, Complex/ and\n   Search/) is imported by lean/MOSPFormalization.lean, so the build really\n   checks it;\n3. the number of `sorry` in Lean *code* (comments and docstrings stripped) is\n   at most the baseline (0: the development has none since Appendix E) plus\n   the entries in allowed_sorries.txt, and no `axiom` declaration exists;\n4. `python -m pytest tests/ -q -x` passes, and so does `pytest` in\n   pathwidth_solver/;\n5. the paper builds with no undefined references (`make -C paper1/latex check`).\n\n    python3 Ralph_Loops/loop0014/gate.py\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport os\nimport re\nimport signal\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\nREPO = HERE.parents[1]\nLEAN = REPO / \"lean\"\nBASELINE_SORRIES = 0\nALLOWED = HERE / \"allowed_sorries.txt\"\nPAPER = REPO / \"paper1\" / \"latex\"\n\n\ndef strip_comments(src: str) -> str:\n    out, i, depth = [], 0, 0\n    while i < len(src):\n        if src.startswith(\"/-\", i):\n            depth += 1; i += 2; continue\n        if depth and src.startswith(\"-/\", i):\n            depth -= 1; i += 2; continue\n        if depth:\n            i += 1; continue\n        if src.startswith(\"--\", i):\n            j = src.find(\"\\n\", i); i = len(src) if j < 0 else j; continue\n        if src[i] == '\"':\n            j = i + 1\n            while j < len(src) and src[j] != '\"':\n                j += 2 if src[j] == \"\\\\\" else 1\n            i = j + 1; continue\n        out.append(src[i]); i += 1\n    return \"\".join(out)\n\n\ndef _run(cmd: list[str], cwd: Path, timeout: float) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:\n    \"\"\"`subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, timeout=timeout)` with two\n    differences, both from the loop0014 item 01 gate hang (2026-11-02): the\n    command runs in its own session, so that on timeout its whole process group\n    is killed (`subprocess.run` kills only the direct child, and the forked\n    pytest worker that had hung outlived the gate by an hour), and the output\n    produced before the timeout travels with the exception, so that `main` can\n    print which test was running.\"\"\"\n    proc = subprocess.Popen(cmd, cwd=cwd, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,\n                            text=True, errors=\"replace\", start_new_session=True)\n    try:\n        out, _ = proc.communicate(timeout=timeout)\n    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:\n        os.killpg(proc.pid, signal.SIGKILL)\n        out, _ = proc.communicate()\n        raise subprocess.TimeoutExpired(cmd, timeout, output=out)\n    return subprocess.CompletedProcess(cmd, proc.returncode, out, \"\")\n\n\n# A test that runs this long has every thread's stack dumped to stderr (pytest's\n# faulthandler plugin); the test goes on, and the dump names it if the gate\n# then times out. The suite takes about 5 min in all.\nFAULTHANDLER = \"faulthandler_timeout=900\"\n\n\ndef _main() -> int:","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CgjcwzkV8daLzeqkodbvzQ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
