ormly with replacement.\nHoeffding’s inequality and a union bound over all 2n assignments give, with probability 1 − 2−Ω(n) ,\n                           |fb(x) − f (x)| ⩽ ε/2    for every x ∈ {0, 1}n .\nThus, if x                       x) ⩾ maxx f (x) − ε. Given an integer threshold b, a Boolean circuit\n         b maximizes fb, then f (b\nwith at most two inputs per gate and size Ok,ε (n) tests whether an assignment satisfies at least b\nsampled clauses. The standard circuit-to-3-CNF reduction [Bar23, Lemmas 15.9–15.10] produces\na formula with Ok,ε (n) variables and clauses that is satisfiable exactly when this threshold can be\nattained. Theorem 1.1, binary search on the threshold, and self-reduction then find an exact sample\noptimizer within the stated bounds. Truncating rejection sampling after O(n + log(n/ε2 )) trials\nper sample gives worst-case poly(n, m, ε−1 ) sampling time and adds at most 2−Ω(n) to the failure\nprobability.                                                                                       □\n   Note that runtime bounds of the form 2o(n) do not transfer under general polynomial-time\n              o(1)\nreductions (2n     would be needed for this), so our result does not imply that general NP problems\nadmit subexponential time algorithms. However we do obtain subexponential time algorithms for\ncounting graph colorings and perfect matchings.\nCorollary 1.3. On simple graphs with v vertices and e edges, the number of independent sets and,\nfor each fixed q ⩾ 2, the number of proper q-colorings can be computed deterministically in 2o(v) time\nand polyq (v, e) space. The number of perfect matchings can be computed deterministically in 2o(v+e)\ntime and poly(v, e) space.\n\f                           THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                 3\n\n\nProof. For G = (V, E), introduce a Boolean variable xu for each u ∈ V . Satisfying assignments of\n                                        ^\n                   