 counting theorem (a hypothesis `SubexpCount k`, never asserted) decides/counts them in 2^{o(n)}·poly, beating the DP once the pathwidth is linear.\n\nRead, in this order:\n1. `lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/Crossover.lean` (new, builds; 13 declarations). Also `lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean` for `SubexpCount`, `runtimeScale`, `IsSubexpExponent`, and `PrimitiveMachine.lean` for the CNF definitions (`SAT.count`, `WidthAtMost`, `encodeCNF`).\n2. `learning/subexp_crossover.py` (new): the encodings (independent set; the threshold `Σ x ≥ t` by a binary adder tree + comparator, Tseitin-encoded; q-colouring; dominating set direct and Tseitin; Max-Cut via edge XORs + threshold; the Cut & Count Hamiltonian-cycle formula whose model count is Σ over 2-factors X of 2^{cc(X)−1}, so that count ≡ #Hamiltonian cycles mod 2; the weighted (isolation-lemma) decision) and the crossover table.\n3. `reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/06_crossover/check_atlas7.log` (the brute-force run: zero failures).\n\nQuestions to answer, each with evidence:\n(a) Is any Lean statement weaker than its docstring claims, vacuous, or circular? In particular: does `indepCount_subexp` really say what the docstring says (the machine input is `encodeCNF (indepCNF G)`, the count tape holds the number of independent sets, time ≤ C(L+1)^d 2^{A ρ(n)} with n the vertex count)? Is `IsSubexpExponent` the right notion? Is `counting_beats_dp_eventually` a trivial consequence of something trivial (e.g. could it be proved with `pathwidth G` replaced by anything ≥ βn — yes, that is intended; say whether the statement is nonetheless the crossover inequality one would want)? Check `#print axioms` yourself if you like (`cd lean && lake env lean <file>`; do NOT run `lake build` — the main agent is the only one allowed to build; `lake env lean` on a scratch file under /tmp is fine).\n(b) Paper proofs the report will make (not yet written; evaluate the mathematics): (i) the adder-tree threshold circuit