 Cygan et al.: (3−ε)^{pw} Connected Vertex Cover etc.) are NOT refuted by the crossover — an algorithm with time 2^{o(n)} is not an algorithm with time (2−ε)^{pw} poly(n) on graphs of small pathwidth — and that the ETH paper itself (reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/01_audit/eth.txt, lines 225–236, §1.1.3) says these bounds also follow from the unrestricted-CNF-SAT hypothesis, which the theorem does not touch. Is that reasoning right? Is there any way the counting theorem DOES refute one of these parameterised lower bounds (e.g. by combining with the DP: run the DP when pw is small, the counting machine when large)?\n\nDo your own brute force to 6 vertices in /tmp with your own code (networkx atlas, itertools) for at least the independent-set count, the threshold formula and the Cut & Count parity — do not reuse the module's checker, but you may import its encoders. Use `nice -n 19`. Write your report to `/home/al/dev/MOSP/reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/06_crossover/refuter.md` (this one file is the exception to \"do not modify files\"): a list of findings, each marked WRONG / GAP / EDITORIAL / OK, with the evidence, then one paragraph \"what I could not check\". Be concise and concrete. Do not spawn subagents.","outputFile":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-al-dev-MOSP/e2f64960-c328-4d7b-b5d1-fcb61f9722bb/tasks/aea14e2d2e4e48aa4.output","canReadOutputFile":true,"canContinueAgent":true},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_011qJdkAuUyVw9TukvPsbhHk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
