 for longest common subsequence and regular-expression matching follow from the\\nOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture (OVC) [BK15, BI16]. OVC asserts that for every fixed ε > 0, no\\nO(N 2−ε poly(d))-time algorithm can decide whether two sets of N Boolean vectors of dimension\\nd contain an orthogonal pair, one vector from each set. The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal\\nVectors shows that SETH implies OVC [Wil05, VW19]. The lower bounds for Fréchet distance,\\ngraph diameter approximation, and the treewidth dependence of Independent Set and Dominating\\nSet also follow from the hypothesis that unrestricted CNF-SAT admits no 2(1−δ)n poly(n, m)-time\\nalgorithm for any constant δ > 0 [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem 1.1 gives a running-time guarantee\\nfor each fixed clause width k, while this hypothesis allows the width to grow with n.\\n   To obtain a faster algorithm for a target problem from our SAT algorithm, we need a reduction in\\nthe other direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT, that controls the number of variables.\\nSet Splitting with sets of size at most k has such a reduction [CDL+ 16, Observation 3.6]: use one\\nBoolean variable per element to specify its side of the split. For each set, one clause requires at least\\n======\\n56:Theorem 1.1. For each fixed k ⩾ 2, there exists a deterministic algorithm that, given a k-CNF F\\n59:                                    (n + m)Ok (1) 2Ok (n log log n/ log n)\\n64:                                           n(log log n)2         n\\n67:Appendix D proves both bounds in Theorem 1.1 by tensor contraction.\\n91:attained. Theorem 1.1, binary search on the threshold, and self-reduction then find an exact sample\\n100:Corollary 1.3. On simple graphs with v vertices and e edges, the number of independent sets and,\\n119:once the edge bits are fixed, so this encoding also preserves the count. Theorem 1.1 gives the stated\\n121:  The fixed-density case of Theorem 1.1 gives a circuit lower bound for ENP , the class of la