{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CginiKSQTQLaao6BEQnLq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 06 is done; the final gate passes on the finished files and PROGRESS.md records it. Not committed, as instructed. Not every item is checked, so no `LOOP_COMPLETE` line.\n\n**What the item produced**\n\n- **The correction that frames every row.** The item's \"SETH-tight bound, now void\" is right about the derivation and wrong about the statement. SETH implies ETH, so under the counting theorem those lower bounds have a false premise. Their statements are not refuted by anything here. A `2^{o(n)}` algorithm is not a `(c − ε)^{pw} · poly` algorithm on graphs of small pathwidth, and the ETH paper's own §1.1.3 says the bounds follow from the unrestricted-CNF-SAT hypothesis, which the theorem leaves alone.\n- **Catalogue and algorithms** (report §6.1–6.2): independent sets, maximum independent set through a binary adder tree (width 3, at most 7 auxiliaries per input measured to 200 inputs), `q`-colourings, dominating set, Max-Cut, odd cycle transversal, SAT by primal pathwidth, and Cut & Count with the dynamic program replaced by the count. The Hamiltonian-cycle formula's model count is the sum over 2-factors of `2^{cc − 1}`, so its parity is computed in `2^{o(n)}` on sparse graphs under the hypothesis. The decision needs isolation-lemma weights, which rest on one remark of the paper, so it is marked conditional rather than claimed.\n- **The crossover** (§6.3): proved as an inequality on exponents, with the table at constant 1. Two honest caveats the table forced: the verified exponent `ρ(n)/n` is above 1 from `n = 50` to about `e^{5,504}`, so its crossover at base 2 is beyond every `n` tabulated; and the table is in the variable count, so at constant 1 only the colouring counts cross inside it.\n- **Lean** `SubexpPathwidth/Crossover.lean`: 24 declarations, no `sorry`, no `axiom`, all on the three standard axioms. `indepCount_subexp` and `coloringCount_subexp` are the two algorithms as `SubexpCount k →` theorems with the encodings' correctness proved; three theorems carry the crossover.\n- **Checks**: every encoding on all 1,252 atlas graphs to 7 vertices, 129,761 evaluations, zero failures. One refuter agent with its own enumerators found two wrong sentences and two gaps, no wrong theorem; all are folded into §6.7.\n\n**Not claimed**: that the hypothesis holds, that any parameterised lower bound is refuted, that the three source papers were read (none is held; recorded for item 11), or that the constant-1 table measures anything."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1808,"cache_read_input_tokens":295188,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1808,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":774,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2f64960-c328-4d7b-b5d1-fcb61f9722bb","uuid":"30a3ca37-111e-45e5-9754-e8eec823fdef","timestamp":"2026-11-04T23:53:54.631Z","request_id":"req_011CginiK6KSMFQFxBZr3uXU"}
